@startuml Factory Patterns - Commission Calculator

' Define the styles (matching strategy pattern style)
skinparam classAttributeIconSize 0
skinparam classFontStyle bold
skinparam classBackgroundColor #f8f9fa
skinparam classBorderColor #dee2e6
skinparam arrowColor #6c757d
skinparam stereotypeCBackgroundColor #e9ecef

' Title
title Factory Patterns - Commission Calculator Implementation

' ==================== SIMPLE FACTORY ====================

  class SimpleFactory <<SimpleFactory>> {
    +{static} createCommissionCalculation(type: String, dealId: String, salesRepId: String, amount: BigDecimal): CommissionCalculation
  }

  class CommissionCalculation <<Product>> {
    -id: String
    -dealId: String
    -salesRepId: String
    -baseCommission: BigDecimal
    -calculatedBy: String
    +recalculate(): void
  }

  SimpleFactory ..> CommissionCalculation : creates >

  note right of SimpleFactory
    <b>Simple Factory (Static Factory)</b>

    Centralizes object creation logic in a
    single static method. Uses conditional
    logic (switch/if) to determine which
    product variant to create.

    <b>Creation Method:</b>
    Based on string parameter ("standard",
    "bonus", "accelerated"), creates and
    configures different CommissionCalculation
    instances with specific initialization.

    <b>Limitation:</b>
    Violates Open/Closed Principle - adding
    new types requires modifying factory code.
  end note


' ==================== FACTORY METHOD ====================
  abstract class DealFactory <<Creator>> {
    #{abstract} createDeal(title: String, value: BigDecimal, salesRepId: String): Deal
    +createDealWithProducts(title: String, value: BigDecimal, salesRepId: String): Deal
    #addDefaultProducts(deal: Deal): void
  }

  class HardwareDealFactory <<ConcreteCreator>> {
    #createDeal(title: String, value: BigDecimal, salesRepId: String): Deal
    #addDefaultProducts(deal: Deal): void
  }

  class SoftwareDealFactory <<ConcreteCreator>> {
    #createDeal(title: String, value: BigDecimal, salesRepId: String): Deal
    #addDefaultProducts(deal: Deal): void
  }

  class ServiceDealFactory <<ConcreteCreator>> {
    #createDeal(title: String, value: BigDecimal, salesRepId: String): Deal
    #addDefaultProducts(deal: Deal): void
  }

  class Deal <<Product>> {
    -id: String
    -title: String
    -value: BigDecimal
    -status: DealStatus
    -salesRepId: String
    -products: List<DealProduct>
    +addProduct(product: DealProduct): void
  }

  DealFactory <|-- HardwareDealFactory
  DealFactory <|-- SoftwareDealFactory
  DealFactory <|-- ServiceDealFactory

  DealFactory ..> Deal : creates >
  HardwareDealFactory ..> Deal : creates HW deals >
  SoftwareDealFactory ..> Deal : creates SW deals >
  ServiceDealFactory ..> Deal : creates SVC deals >

  note right of DealFactory
    <b>Factory Method (Creator)</b>

    Defines an abstract interface for creating
    objects, but lets subclasses decide which
    concrete class to instantiate.

    <b>Template Method Integration:</b>
    createDealWithProducts() is a template
    method that calls:
    1. createDeal() - factory method
    2. addDefaultProducts() - hook method

    <b>Benefit:</b>
    Adheres to Open/Closed Principle - new
    deal types are added by creating new
    subclasses, not modifying existing code.
  end note

  note bottom of HardwareDealFactory
    <b>Concrete Creator</b>

    Creates Deal with ID prefix "HW-"
    and adds hardware-specific products:
    • Laptop (HW-LAPTOP)
    • Monitor (HW-MONITOR)

    Each factory encapsulates the
    creation logic for its product type.
  end note


' ==================== ABSTRACT FACTORY ====================

  interface CommissionFactory <<AbstractFactory>> {
    +createPlanCreator(): CommissionPlanCreator
    +createCalculator(): CommissionCalculator
  }

  class StandardCommissionFactory <<ConcreteFactory>> {
    +createPlanCreator(): CommissionPlanCreator
    +createCalculator(): CommissionCalculator
  }

  class PremiumCommissionFactory <<ConcreteFactory>> {
    +createPlanCreator(): CommissionPlanCreator
    +createCalculator(): CommissionCalculator
  }

  interface CommissionPlanCreator <<AbstractProduct>> {
    +createCommissionPlan(name: String, description: String): CommissionPlan
  }

  interface CommissionCalculator <<AbstractProduct>> {
    +calculateCommission(deal: Deal, salesRep: User, plan: CommissionPlan): CommissionCalculation
  }

  class StandardCommissionPlanCreator <<ConcreteProduct>> {
    +createCommissionPlan(name: String, description: String): CommissionPlan
  }

  class PremiumCommissionPlanCreator <<ConcreteProduct>> {
    +createCommissionPlan(name: String, description: String): CommissionPlan
  }

  class StandardCommissionCalculator <<ConcreteProduct>> {
    +calculateCommission(deal: Deal, salesRep: User, plan: CommissionPlan): CommissionCalculation
  }

  class PremiumCommissionCalculator <<ConcreteProduct>> {
    +calculateCommission(deal: Deal, salesRep: User, plan: CommissionPlan): CommissionCalculation
  }

  class CommissionPlan <<DomainProduct>> {
    -id: String
    -name: String
    -rules: List<CommissionRule>
    -effectiveStartDate: LocalDate
    -effectiveEndDate: LocalDate
  }

  CommissionFactory <|.. StandardCommissionFactory
  CommissionFactory <|.. PremiumCommissionFactory

  CommissionPlanCreator <|.. StandardCommissionPlanCreator
  CommissionPlanCreator <|.. PremiumCommissionPlanCreator

  CommissionCalculator <|.. StandardCommissionCalculator
  CommissionCalculator <|.. PremiumCommissionCalculator

  StandardCommissionFactory ..> StandardCommissionPlanCreator : creates >
  StandardCommissionFactory ..> StandardCommissionCalculator : creates >
  PremiumCommissionFactory ..> PremiumCommissionPlanCreator : creates >
  PremiumCommissionFactory ..> PremiumCommissionCalculator : creates >

  CommissionPlanCreator ..> CommissionPlan : creates >
  CommissionCalculator ..> CommissionCalculation : creates >

  note right of CommissionFactory
    <b>Abstract Factory Interface</b>

    Declares methods for creating families
    of related products. Each method returns
    an abstract product type.

    <b>Product Family Concept:</b>
    Creates multiple related products that
    are designed to work together:
    • CommissionPlanCreator
    • CommissionCalculator

    Ensures consistency within product families.
  end note

  note bottom of StandardCommissionFactory
    <b>Concrete Factory</b>

    Creates STANDARD family:
    • StandardCommissionPlanCreator
      (5% base rate, no bonuses)
    • StandardCommissionCalculator
      (Basic calculation algorithm)

    Products from same factory are
    guaranteed to be compatible.
  end note

  note bottom of PremiumCommissionFactory
    <b>Concrete Factory</b>

    Creates PREMIUM family:
    • PremiumCommissionPlanCreator
      (8% base rate)
    • PremiumCommissionCalculator
      (Enhanced algorithm with 10% bonus)

    Higher rates and additional features
    compared to standard family.
  end note

  note right of CommissionPlanCreator
    <b>Abstract Product A</b>

    Defines interface for creating
    commission plans. Each concrete
    implementation creates plans with
    different rate structures.
  end note

  note right of CommissionCalculator
    <b>Abstract Product B</b>

    Defines interface for calculating
    commissions. Works with plans created
    by the same factory to ensure
    compatibility.
  end note


' Legend
note right of CommissionCalculator
  <b>Factory Pattern Comparison:</b>

  <b>Simple Factory:</b>
  • Single factory class with static creation method
  • Uses conditional logic (switch/if) to select product type
  • Simplest pattern, but violates Open/Closed Principle
  • Best for: Limited, stable product types

  <b>Factory Method:</b>
  • Abstract creator class with factory method
  • Subclasses implement factory method to create specific products
  • Follows Open/Closed Principle
  • Can integrate with Template Method pattern
  • Best for: Single product type with multiple variants

  <b>Abstract Factory:</b>
  • Creates families of related products
  • Multiple factory methods (one per product type)
  • Ensures product family consistency
  • More complex but provides highest level of abstraction
  • Best for: Multiple related products that must work together

  <b>Pattern Selection Guide:</b>
  • Use Simple Factory for quick, straightforward object creation
  • Use Factory Method when you need extensibility for one product type
  • Use Abstract Factory when you need to create families of related objects

  <b>Common Benefits Across All Patterns:</b>
  • Encapsulates object creation logic
  • Reduces coupling between client code and concrete classes
  • Makes code more maintainable and testable
  • Provides meaningful names for creation operations
end note

@enduml